+        // Strip a leading namespace-qualifier path down to the bare identifier,
+        // as a final fallback (e.g. "REX::W32::CRITICAL_SECTION" ->
+        // "CRITICAL_SECTION", "RE::ActorValue" -> "ActorValue"). TypePool
+        // registers every struct/union/enum/typedef under its BARE
+        // (non-namespace-qualified) name -- see SourceParser.parseStruct's use
+        // of structCursor.spelling(), which never includes namespace
+        // qualification (namespaces only ever affect CategoryPath, not the
+        // registered name). But clang's own Type.spelling() DOES include full
+        // namespace qualification whenever a field/base type is referenced
+        // from a DIFFERENT namespace than where it's declared -- extremely
+        // common in this codebase, e.g. any RE:: class using a REX::W32::*
+        // Win32 stand-in type. Confirmed via a debug trace: RE::
+        // BSCriticalSection's own member is declared with type spelling
+        // "REX::W32::CRITICAL_SECTION", which never matched the pool's
+        // registered "CRITICAL_SECTION" key, permanently blocking
+        // BSCriticalSection's dependency check -- and cascading into every
+        // class that (transitively) depends on it, e.g. RE::AbstractHeap,
+        // RE::AIProcess. Deliberately does NOT apply to template
+        // instantiations (names containing "<"): those go through the
+        // separate inline-embedding mechanism in SourceParser (patches
+        // 0003/0005), and a naive last-"::"-segment split would incorrectly
+        // cut through a namespace-qualified template argument (e.g.
+        // "RE::NiPointer<Actor>" must not become "Actor>"). See
+        // patches/0006-fix-cross-namespace-type-references.md.
+        if (!s.contains("<")) {
